32	// Undo / redo.
33	//
34	// There is exactly one restorable state in this product: a successful build's
35	// `filesSnapshot`. Nothing else is versioned — a Brand or SEO save overwrites
36	// the draft files in place, and no route can write an arbitrary tree back. So
37	// undo is defined over CHECKPOINTS, and this comment is the honest statement of
38	// what that does and does not give you:
39	//
40	//   * Undoing a build restores the checkpoint before it.
41	//   * Undoing a manual edit (Brand / SEO) restores the checkpoint it was made
42	//     on top of — so the edit is undone, but as a side effect of putting the
43	//     whole tree back, not because the edit itself was recorded.
44	//   * A manual edit therefore CANNOT BE REDONE. Nothing captured it. Redo says
45	//     so rather than offering a button that would silently do something else.
46	//
47	// `cursor` is the checkpoint the draft matches. Within a session it is a fact:
48	// every move is one this component performed itself. Across a reload it is
49	// seeded from an exact server-side tree comparison (page.tsx) that can answer
50	// only one question — "does the draft match the NEWEST checkpoint?" — because
51	// answering it for every checkpoint would mean loading every snapshot.
52	//
53	// So a reloaded page knows either "the draft is at the newest checkpoint" or
54	// "the draft is somewhere else", and NOT where else. That is why the
55	// off-checkpoint label says "restore the last build" rather than "go back":
56	// after a restore-then-reload the newest checkpoint is forwards, not
57	// backwards, and a button claiming otherwise would be wrong. Restoring the
58	// last build is a true description of the click in both cases.
59	//
